<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Susu people</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Soso</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Susu are a Mande-speaking people of coastal <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guinea">Guinea</a> and north-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sierra-leone">Sierra Leone</a>. Moving towards the coast from the Futa Jallon highlands, partly under pressure from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fula">Fula</a>, they became traders and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rice">rice</a> farmers and largely embraced <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a>. Their language serves as a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lingua-franca">lingua franca</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/conakry">Conakry</a> and along the Guinean coast.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/susu-people.jpg" alt="Susu people" width="320" height="226" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:SoussousGuiembeBalafon.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The Susu speak Sosoxui, a language of the Soso-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yalunka">Yalunka</a> branch of the Mande languages, closely related to Yalunka and influenced by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mandinka">Mandinka</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soninke">Soninke</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kpelle-people">Kpelle</a>. They are believed to have Soninke roots and to be connected with the medieval <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sosso-empire">Sosso Empire</a>. One tradition holds that they were a section of the Soninke who migrated out of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ghana-empire">Wagadou</a>, at first a clan of blacksmiths. In the twelfth century, as the ancient Ghana Empire declined, they are said to have moved south and founded a capital, Soso, in the mountainous region of Koulikoro. They were once ruled by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sumanguru-kante">Sumanguru Kanté</a> and afterwards by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mali-empire">Mali Empire</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>In the fifteenth century, as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mali-empire">Mali Empire</a> broke up, the Susu moved west to the Fouta Djallon plateau. Portuguese writers of the fifteenth and sixteenth centuries, including Valentim Fernandes and Duarte Pacheco Pereira, give some of the earliest written records of the Soso and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yalunka">Yalunka</a>. They describe a kingdom called Jalo Concho, and record that the two peoples were animists with organised <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caste">caste</a> systems, active in regional trade and at war with the Mané.</p><p>In the eighteenth century <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fula">Fula</a> invaders separated the Susu from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yalunka">Yalunka</a>. The Fula formed an Islamic state in the Fouta Djallon and, from the 1720s, justified enslaving the non-Islamic by a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jihad">jihad</a>, according to the historian Ismail Rashid. Under this pressure the Susu converted to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a> in the seventeenth and eighteenth centuries and moved south and west to the coastal plains, absorbing peoples such as the Nalu, Baga and Landouma.</p><p>Their coastal <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chiefdom">chiefdoms</a> were decentralised. Dubréka was a prominent Susu chiefdom in the eighteenth and nineteenth centuries, and its ruler Balé Demba concluded a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protectorate">protectorate</a> agreement with French authorities in 1880. Kindia, inland, was another political and commercial centre. When the colonial powers divided the region in the late nineteenth century, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guinea">Guinea</a> regions where Susu lived became part of the French empire, while the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sierra-leone">Sierra Leone</a> regions where Temne was spoken went to the British.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>Susu society is patrilineal, and more than 99 per cent of Susu are Muslim. Extended families live close together, and polygyny is practised within Islamic law. Like other <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mande-peoples">Mande peoples</a>, the Susu have <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caste">castes</a> of artisans, among them smiths, carpenters, musicians, bards, jewellers and leatherworkers. Girls are prepared for adulthood through the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sande-society">Bondo</a> society. Most Susu are farmers, growing <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rice">rice</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/millet">millet</a>, and they are also known as traders and blacksmiths. Women make <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/palm-oil">palm oil</a>, and many also trade in garden vegetables. In the Susu language the word for woman is guinea, from which the country's name derives.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Susu people” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Most people were Muslim Fulbe, Maninka and <b>Susu</b>, and the Futa Jallon remained a centre of Islamic learning.</blockquote><a href="/state/french-guinea">French Guinea</a></li></ul></section>
